{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.087,
            0.846,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "？ Take a few more examples of multiplication of 3 integers and check this property. What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.132,
            0.847,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": "We can see that the product is the same when we ‘group’ the multiplications in these two ways. So, integer multiplication is associative, just like integer addition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.195,
            0.608,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "In general, for any three integers a, b, and c,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.399,
            0.216,
            0.598,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": "a \\times ( b \\times c ) = ( a \\times b ) \\times c .",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.236,
            0.846,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "In the expression $5 \\times - 3 \\times 4 ,$ try to multiply 5 and 4 first and then multiply the product with – 3: $( 5 \\times 4 ) \\times - 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.357,
            0.275,
            0.638,
            0.293
          ],
          "content": "5 \\times 4 = 20, \\mathrm{and} 20 \\times -3 = -60.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.296,
            0.476,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "This also gives the same product."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.323,
            0.846,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "？ Are there orders in which $5 \\times - 3 \\times 4$ can be evaluated? Will the product be the same in all these cases?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.372,
            0.846,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "？ Multiply the expression 25 × – 6 × 12 in all the different orders and check if the product is the same in all cases."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.417,
            0.846,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": "The product remains the same when 3 or more numbers are multiplied in any order."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.469,
            0.64,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "Look at the following series of multiplications:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.367,
            0.494,
            0.48,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "-1 \\times -1 = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.367,
            0.514,
            0.54,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "-1 \\times -1 \\times -1 = -1",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.534,
            0.574,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": "-1 \\times -1 \\times -1 \\times -1 = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.552,
            0.618,
            0.571
          ],
          "content": "-1 \\times -1 \\times -1 \\times -1 \\times -1 = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.576,
            0.766,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": "When –1 is multiplied 2 or 4 times the product is positive. When it is multiplied 3 or 5 times the product is negative. <u>Can you generalise these statements further?</u>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.651,
            0.802,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "? Using this understanding of multiplication of many integers, can you give a simple rule to find the sign of the product of many integers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.633,
            0.906,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.703,
            0.847,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "？ Now, consider the expression $5 \\times ( 4 + ( - 2 ) )$ . As in the case of positive integers, is this expression equal to $5 \\times 4 + 5 \\times ( - 2) ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.748,
            0.847,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "We see that it does. Recall that we call this property the distributive property."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.794,
            0.848,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "？ Check if the distributive property holds for $( - 2 ) \\times ( 4 + ( - 3 ) )$ (that is, if this expression equals $( - 2 ) \\times 4 + ( - 2 ) \\times ( - 3 ) )$ , and for a few other such expressions of your choice."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.858,
            0.847,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "What do you observe? We see that the distributive property seems to hold for integers, as well. Will this always happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.934,
            0.187,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "40"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
